{% set local_problem %}
<p>Athens' tree coverage is a defining feature — towering oaks lining streets near the courthouse square, mature maples shading <a href="/recent-projects/">neighborhoods around Athens State University</a>, pecan trees in backyards throughout the county. That canopy provides shade, character, and natural beauty that newer subdivisions can't replicate.</p>

<p>But every one of those trees drops debris directly into your gutters. In fall, it's a deluge — large oak leaves, smaller maple leaves, pecan shells and stems. In spring, it's seeds, pollen, and helicopter pods. Year-round, it's twigs from wind and storms. Athens homeowners often find themselves cleaning gutters 3-4 times per year just to keep up.</p>

<p>Many try DIY cleaning — weekends spent on ladders, sometimes dangerous ones reaching second-story gutters. Others pay $150-200 per professional cleaning. That's $600-800 annually for a chore that never ends. Do that for 3-4 years and you've spent more than quality guards cost.</p>

<p>Meanwhile, between cleanings, clogged gutters overflow during heavy rains. Water cascades down siding, pools around foundations, and on Limestone County's clay soil, that pooled water doesn't absorb — it finds its way toward your basement.</p>

<p><strong>Our strong opinion:</strong> Don't buy foam gutter inserts from the hardware store. We've pulled thousands of feet of failed foam out of <a href="/recent-projects/">Athens gutters</a>. They decompose within 18-24 months, trap debris inside instead of keeping it out, and become part of the clog. Spend on micro-mesh or don't bother at all.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are protective covers that fit over or inside your gutters. They let water through while blocking leaves, seeds, twigs, and debris. Simple concept — but product selection matters enormously.</p>

<p><strong>What works for Athens:</strong> Micro-mesh guards with surgical-grade stainless steel screen. The holes are tiny — around 50 microns — blocking everything from big oak leaves to fine pecan shell fragments. Water sheets through; debris sits on top and blows or washes off.</p>

<p><strong>What doesn't work here:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Foam inserts — decompose in Alabama humidity, become part of the clog within 2 years</li>
<li>Plastic snap-in screens — warp in summer heat, don't block fine debris</li>
<li>Brush-style guards — pecan shells and small debris embed in bristles, nearly impossible to clean</li>
</ul>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">Athens homes</a> with mixed tree coverage — the typical situation near downtown and around the university — micro-mesh handles everything. For homes with only large-leaf trees and no pecans, screen guards can work at lower cost.</p>

<p><strong>The realistic expectation:</strong> Quality guards reduce cleaning needs by 90%+. You might brush surface debris off once a year. That's not "never clean again" but it's dramatically better than scooping decomposed sludge four times a year.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed guards on hundreds of <a href="/recent-projects/">Athens homes</a> and learned exactly which products work for the area's specific mix of oak, maple, and pecan debris. We're not pushing the most expensive option or whatever has the best margin. We recommend what actually works here.</p>

<p>As local contractors serving Limestone County for 20+ years, we stand behind the work. If something isn't performing, we're 25 minutes away and we'll make it right.</p>

<p><strong>Warning:</strong> Be skeptical of gutter guard companies offering "lifetime" cleaning guarantees. Read the fine print. Most exclude "maintenance debris" — defined broadly enough to cover almost everything. We're upfront: guards reduce cleaning 90%+, not 100%. Occasional surface maintenance happens. That's the honest reality.</p>
{% endset %}

